  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nCurrently a very elongated ridge exists across the southern \nCONUS with zonal to WNW mid level flow in the northern CONUS. As\nwe progress through the week, ridging over the four corners \ninto Texas panhandle will amplify. At the same time PNW \ntroughing begins to turn flow more zonal over us as the ridge \ngets pushed east. By Thursday SW flow looks to dominate the \nwestern CONUS bringing pacific moisture over the Rockies with \nembedded shortwaves. Certainly still differences remaining among\nclusters but the potential for a wet late week/weekend \ncontinues to increase. Current guidance is now favoring western \nareas (eastern North Dakota) over eastern areas (northwest \nMinnesota) with a 60% chance for over half an inch in the next \nseven days vs only 40% chance out east. As much of this rain \nlooks to be more convective and thunderstorm driven vs a slow \nsoaking rain totals will likley vary greatly over short \ndistances.\n\n- Severe threat today\n\nThis morning MUCAPE of 1000-2000 j/kg atop a nearly stationary \nsurface boundary led to the formation of a lone supercell that \ntracked from north of Jamestown towards Fort Ransom dropping up \nto baseball sized hail along the way. Overall this was mostly a \nsurprise and not in the forecast given the skinny CAPE, however\nthe very favorable straight hodos and eff shear of around 50kts\nat least helped explain it a little. Looking ahead to this \nevening CAMS largely maintain the instability across the area \nbut fail to convect despite an overall capable environment \nshould a forcing mechanism exist. The chance for a storm this \nevening is likely less than 10% but if one goes, something \nsimilar to this morning is certainly possible. By the overnight \nthere is higher confidence in storms forming west of Bismarck \nand moving east reaching the James/Sheyenne river valleys around\n4-5am. Storms still look to be elevated and a primary hail \nthreat, but cant 100% rule out damaging winds. This threat \nwould persist into the mid morning hours mainly for southeast \nNorth Dakota and perhaps west central Minnesota is storms made \nit that far east.\n\n- Severe threat Wednesday\n\nA more pronounced shortwave will roll across the Dakotas \nWednesday evening bringing forcing for widespread convection. \nthunderstorms may occasionally have large hail and gusty winds \nbut overall this looks to be more of a general \nthunderstorm/widespread rain event for eastern North Dakota. \nAmounts will generally will be a quarter to half inch but \nisolated totals in excess of 1\" are possible.",
